hey guys is Vadim and welcome to max  Tech when it comes to buying an iPad pro  you've got the choice between the 2017  10.5 inch iPad pro for 649 dollars or  the brand-new 2018 iPad pro for at least  seven hundred and ninety nine dollars  today we'll be comparing them to see  which iPad pro model is the right choice  for you starting with the design the  2018 iPad pro features a brand new  design with flat edges instead of the  old ipad pro's curved and chamfered  edges I personally love the new design  except that the flat edges do feel a  little bit weird in the hands until you  get used to it the home button is now  gone so the top and bottom bezels are  slimmed down making for nice even bezels  all around the curved edge display I  also love that the front of the new iPad  pro is now blacked out just looking at  them side by side it's easy to see that  the old iPad pro looks outdated compared  to the new iPad pro this alone might be  enough of a reason to buy the new one  instead with a home button now gone the  2018 pro gets face ID instead and let me  tell you it works great since you can  use it in practically any orientation  the displays themselves actually aren't  any different but they both look amazing  compared to smaller iPads they're both  super bright and support Apple's pro  motion technology which allows the  display to refresh at up to 120 times a  second making animations and navigation  buttery smooth unfortunately the new  iPad pro is the first one ever to get  rid of the headphone jack but Apple does  sell a $9 adapter so it's not too big of  a deal the new pro finally switches over  to USBC allowing you to connect USB see  accessories but you still can't connect  external storage drives to me the USB C  port is nice because you can easily  charge the new iPad pro using a USB C  macbook charger one huge benefit is that  the new pro comes with an 18 watt  charger instead of the old 12 watt  charger that came on basically every  ipad so it's gonna charge much quicker  with the new iPad pro comes a new Apple  pencil which charges wirelessly by  attaching it to the side of the iPad pro  this new method of charging is a  thousand times better than the old plug  into the Lightning port method this will  make sure your pencil is always topped  off in the magnetic connection is pretty  strong so it won't easily get lost the  new Apple pencil is $30 more expensive  than the old one but  think it's worth it since you wouldn't  want to buy the old pencil and then have  to buy the new one in the future anyway  the new pencil looks better and it also  gets a new DoubleTap feature that can  instantly switch between tools so to me  there's no comparison whichever one you  choose they both perform incredibly well  with very minimal lack compared to other  iPads that lack promotion technology now  let's compare performance starting with  Geekbench for cpu benchmark we got a 90  percent higher score on the new iPad pro  in the graphics test we only saw an  increase of around 36% but when we tried  and Tutu's graphics test we had a score  that was over twice as high as a 10.5  inch iPad pro now that's absolutely  insane considering how powerful the ten  point five inch pro already was we then  run base marks metal graphics test in  the 11 inch iPad pro again scored over  twice as high running GFX benches 1080p  Manhattan test the new 11 inch iPad pro  scored 133 percent higher than the 10.5  inch model substantially more than  double the score we decided to run a  more realistic test exporting and edited  one-minute 4k 60p video using Adobe rush  the 11 inch iPad pro finished exporting  in only 2 minutes and 56 seconds  compared to 6 minutes and 10 seconds on  the 10.5 inch iPad pro that's again more  than twice as fast  finally testing photo editing which  doesn't really use much of the graphics  chip we exported 50 edited JPEG images  in Adobe Lightroom cc the 11 inch iPad  pro finished in 2 minutes and 33 seconds  while the 10.5 inch iPad pro finished in  2 minutes and 58 seconds it's easy to  see just how much more powerful the new  11 inch iPad pro is at least four  graphics intensive tasks however to be  completely honest you probably won't  notice that big of a difference only  because the 10.5 inch iPad pro was  already able to handle most iOS apps  with ease to really see the new iPad pro  shine we're gonna have to wait for even  more power demanding apps and software  we decided to compare the cameras on  both ipad pro's and as you can see the  new Pro turns out much better looking  photos the quality has improved as well  as dynamic range now the biggest  difference is seen on the front end  thanks to face ID in the true depth  sensor the new iPad pro can out  portrait selfies which look many times  better than any selfie you can take on  the 10.5 inch iPad pro this also means  that you get features like an emoji and  momochi which I've come to realize don't  really matter much at all since I've  only used them once since I got my  iPhone 10's max months ago lastly let's  talk about bengay honestly I don't think  it's a big deal at all the old iPad pro  is definitely more durable but we've  experienced zero issues so far with the  new one overall there's absolutely no  comparison between the 10.5 inch iPad  pro and the 2018 iPad pro the new one is  so much better in almost every way  there's only one thing stopping me from  recommending the new iPad pro to  literally everyone looking to buy an  iPad pro and that's the price at $799  for the pro in $130 for the Apple pencil  it's simply unaffordable for a lot of  people so my conclusion is this if you  have the extra cash and you can buy  either one definitely by the 2018 iPad  pro instead of saving some cash on the  10.5 inch iPad pro you won't regret your  purchase  now if the new iPad pro looks appealing  but it's way out of your price range try  to find a used 10.5 inch iPad pro that  comes with an Apple pencil already if  you'd rather not  I'd recommend waiting until March when  Apple will probably release a new budget  iPad thanks for watching guys and make  sure to subscribe so you don't miss out  on more max tech videos we'll see you in  the next one
 

